    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Celtic religion was polytheistic: the names of over two hundred deities survive, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>, Toutatis, Taranis, Cernunnos, Epona and Belenos, and several gods were seen as threefold.</li><li>Its priests were the druids, and its rites were held in sacred groves called nemetons, at springs and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> temples, with offerings, often of weapons, thrown into rivers, lakes and bogs.</li><li>Almost everything known of it comes from archaeology, from Greek and Roman writers who were often hostile, and from medieval Irish and Welsh tales written down by Christian monks.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In Ireland the satire of a gifted poet was a serious curse: the filid, visionary poets and keepers of lore, were also magicians, for Irish magic was bound up with poetry.</p></aside>
    </section>

