    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancient Celtic religion, also called Celtic polytheism or Celtic paganism, was the religion of the ancient Celtic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, one of a larger group of polytheistic Indo-European religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Europe. It descended from Proto-Celtic paganism, itself derived from Proto-Indo-European religion, and many Celtic deities have cognates elsewhere: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigantia">Brigantia</a> with the Roman Aurora and the Vedic Ushas, the Welsh Arianrhod with the Greek Selene, and the Irish Danu with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Danu, whose name is shared by rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the Don and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>.</p><p>The gods worshipped varied by region and over time, but scholars find broad similarities among them and “a basic religious homogeneity” among the Celtic peoples. The earliest monumental stone sculptures, the Warrior of Hirschlanden and the “Glauberg Prince” from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, date to the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, though it is far from clear that they show deities. Such sculpture appears mostly where trade brought close contact with the classical world, and wooden figures may once have been more common.</p><p>Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> wrote nothing down about their religion, it is pieced together from archaeology, Greek and Roman writers and early Christian literature. The archaeologist Barry Cunliffe, borrowing a phrase from Proinsias MacCana, called the evidence “fertile chaos”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lugus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugus.jpg" alt="Lugus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PenalbaDeVillastar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Talskubilos, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Celtic religion was polytheistic. Over two hundred names of deities survive, though many may be other names or titles of the same god, and some gods were worshipped only by one tribe or in one area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> seem to have had a father god of the tribe and of the dead, Toutatis probably being one of his names, and a mother goddess of the land and fertility, perhaps called Matrona, who could also be a war goddess, as Andraste was. Taranis was a sky god of thunder, the wheel and the bull; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a> a god of skill and craft; Sirona and Borvo healers tied to sacred springs. Cernunnos, the horned god, and Epona, goddess of horses, were widely known, and triplicity was common, as in the Three Mothers.</p><p>Offerings were made throughout the landscape: in sacred groves called nemeta, at springs and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> temples. Weapons and treasures were thrown into rivers, lakes and bogs or buried in shafts. Animals, almost always livestock or working animals, were sacrificed, and Greek and Roman writers describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, accounts historians treat with caution.</p><p>Grave goods of food, weapons and ornaments suggest a belief in life after death; some scholars see the otherworld of later Celtic myth as the old afterlife, though there is no direct evidence. The Insular Celts kept four seasonal festivals: Beltaine, Lughnasadh, Samhain and Imbolc.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cu-chulainn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cu-chulainn.jpg" alt="Cú Chulainn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cu-chulainn">Cú Chulainn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuslayshound.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stephen Reid, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No native religious texts survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, wrote the earliest and fullest account of the druids and named the Gaulish gods by Roman equivalents: Mercury, he said, was the most widely venerated, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>, Mars and Jupiter, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> believed they descended from a god of the dead he likened to Dīs Pater. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, Lucan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> added rites and sacrifices, but such writers saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> as “barbarians”, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had reason to make them seem uncivilised.</p><p>The Irish and Welsh tales were written down by Christian monks several centuries after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> became dominant, by writers who may have been hostile to the old paganism or ignorant of it. In them the gods become heroes with supernatural powers; in Ireland they are the Tuatha Dé Danann. The Irish evidence is thought older and less mixed with foreign material than the Welsh. Opinion is divided on whether the tales keep oral tradition preserved by bards or were created in the monasteries, and Ronald Hutton warned against treating every hero as a former god: “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cu-chulainn">Cú Chulainn</a> is no more a former god than Superman is.”</p><p>The oldest attested Celtic calendar is the Coligny calendar of the 2nd century, from Roman Gaul.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara.jpg" alt="Hill of Tara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Hill of Tara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Municipal_District_of_Ashbourne_-_Hill_of_Tara_-_2025-09-30_10-53-27_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paulzygmunt, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtic peoples practised their religion across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Belgica</a> to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Celtiberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Their sanctuaries varied: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> temples within rectangular enclosures in Gaul, Belgica and Britain, ditched enclosures called viereckschanzen in southern Germany, and circular sites in Ireland, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Hill of Tara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emain-macha">Navan Fort</a> are the most notable.</p><p>Roman conquest changed the religion wherever it reached. After the conquest of Gaul in 58–51 BCE and of southern Britain in 43 CE, Celtic religion there blended with Roman religion into a syncretic Gallo-Roman faith, with gods such as Lenus Mars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-grannus">Apollo Grannus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulis-minerva">Sulis Minerva</a>, while Epona was taken into Roman worship. Iron Age sacred sites were often reused for Roman temples, and the Romano-Celtic temples of the northwest followed earlier Celtic plans.</p><p>The religion then gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> converted gradually from the 3rd century. After Roman rule in Britain ended around 410, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> paganism replaced Celtic paganism over much of what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Celtic peoples of Britain and Ireland converted gradually from the 5th century. In Ireland, the main Celtic land never conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the church had relegated the druids to irrelevancy by the early 7th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek and Roman writers, among them Caesar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, describe the druids as magico-religious specialists held in high esteem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Caesar says they were concerned with “divine worship, the due performance of sacrifices, private or public, and the interpretation of ritual questions”, and that they officiated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a> such as the wicker man burnings, a claim modern scholars have questioned. Pliny wrote of druids cutting mistletoe from a sacred oak with a golden sickle. Irish sources portray them as sorcerers who cursed, divined and opposed the coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Peter Berresford Ellis likened them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, Anne Ross to tribal shamans, and Ronald Hutton held that “we can know virtually nothing of certainty about the ancient Druids”.</p><p>Poets formed another learned order. In Ireland the filid were visionary poets, lorekeepers and magicians, above the lesser bards; poets of every grade composed eulogy and satire and kept the genealogies of their patrons. In Roman Gaul the bards disappeared, but in Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> they survived into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>Under Roman rule, Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> suppressed the druidic hierarchies and replaced them with Roman-style priesthoods. Among the Insular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> oaths were sworn by the tribal gods and by the land, the sea and the sky.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Christianisation transformed Celtic society. In Ireland the church stamped out animal sacrifice, and Cormac’s Glossary (c. 900) says Saint Patrick banished the rites of the filid that involved offerings to “demons”. Yet the filid lived in easy harmony with the clergy and kept much of their tradition, status and privilege. The sacred marriage of the king with the goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was purged early from royal inauguration but stayed in literature for centuries.</p><p>Much survived in folklore. The scholar Nagy noted that Gaelic oral tradition was remarkably conservative, with tales told in the 19th century almost as in the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and mythology based on the old traditions was still common knowledge in Celtic-speaking cultures then; the Celtic Revival collected it. Beltane is still kept, the Puck Fair at Killorglin seems to survive from Lughnasadh, and pilgrimages to holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, the clootie wells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, and well dressing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a> continue. On the Inishkea Islands, Celtic pagan rituals were seemingly performed well into the 19th century.</p><p>In the 20th century Celtic paganism became the basis of Celtic neopaganism, from Reconstructionists who try to practise the ancient religion as accurately as possible to eclectic groups, the best known being Neo-Druidry. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> the bardic order, revived and codified by the poet and forger Iolo Morganwg, lives on in the eisteddfods.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Celtic religion was the polytheistic faith of the ancient Celtic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>, Taranis, Cernunnos and Epona, druid priests, sacred groves and offerings in water. Known only through archaeology, often hostile Greek and Roman writers and medieval Christian texts, it blended with Roman religion after the conquest and gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, from the 3rd century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the 5th in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland.</em></p>

